      "source": [
        "Well this was a learning experience! I thought that separating the genders would make it easier to identify passenger characteristics correlated with survival, but I was never able to get the AUC of the exclusively female (0.84) or exclusively male (AUC = 0.71) decision tree models to beat my combined gender model (AUC = 0.8837). I guess that's because gender was such an important factor in determining survival, and when you take it out of the equation, what might be correlated with survival becomes harder to find.\n",
        "\n",
        "I thought it was really interesting how when I was working with the men specifically, I repeatedly ran into a situation where my model predicted incorrectly that all surviving men actually died! I couldn't find any criteria that would with any kind of consistence classify them as having survived. Even in the mixed-gender model, men who did survive were very often misclassified.\n",
        "\n",
        "This just further supports the reality that the number one predictor of whether you would have survived the Titanic sinking is whether or not you were female.\n",
        "\n",
        "I also learned that adding more depth to a decision tree model doesn't necessarily make it better. When I lowered the depth of my model from 4 to 3, for example, my AUC went from 0.8825 to 0.8837. I know that the AUC will vary depending on the sample, but using this particular training and test data, reducing the depth of the model made it very slightly better."
